-U01-L08","ENG7-U01-L09","ENG7-U01-L10"],"nearestExistingAssessment":["ENG7-U01-DA-28","ENG7-U01-MA-28"],"nearestBatch":["ENG7-U01-DB-28","ENG7-U01-MB-28"],"newStimulusInterpretation":"Attribute a two-step decision method rather than confuse it with testing movement or remembering the dramatic purpose.","completeSolution":"Speaker Three suggests settling shape before discussing colours.","guideRelationship":"The existing Unit 1 guide teaches this procedure. Its worked examples do not give this complete contextual answer. The procedure remains familiar; no independent new reasoning coverage is claimed.","protectionJudgment":"Reserve this exact stimulus and its derivatives to this form. Fresh interpretation is an automated-author judgment; human content, SME and exposure qualification remain pending. Do not show its source or solution in Practice or Chapter Review.","independentNewReasoningClaimed":false,"semanticReviewer":"Codex automated author; independent human review pending"},"source":{"id":"ENG7-U01-MC-AUDIO-B","kind":"listening","format":"script","familyId":"3ca844ae-c14e-5420-ae24-3e0519a0316d","title":"Listening source MC-B","locale":"en","text":"narrator: Listen to three students suggesting ways to settle a disagreement about a costume for a school play. You will hear each speaker''s number.\n\nspeaker_1: Speaker One. We could try both ideas in a short rehearsal. Then we would know which one lets the actor move more easily, instead of only guessing.\n\nspeaker_2: Speaker Two. Let''s remember what the character needs to do in the scene. We all want the audience to understand the character, even though we prefer different designs.\n\nspeaker_3: Speaker Three. Perhaps we can decide on the shape first and discuss the colours afterwards. Trying to settle every detail at once is making the discussion harder.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}}]'::jsonb) s join public.items i on i.id=(s->>'i